{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788132749,"nonce":"07kcb2WiKnEsMVFXJP7IJ0dQpE0=","title":"Depends: 1.3 (the advance loop). Wave 1, p1. Found by the action-surface audit,","message":"session 4, and **verified line by line by the orchestrator before filing.**\n\n\nAn advance can stack a force-pausing modal window and keep ticking under it, and\nfrom that moment every timing-out letter in the game silently expires without\never opening. Nothing in AutoRimmer notices.\n\nThe chain, all confirmed in the decompiled 1.6 source:\n\n1. `TickManager.cs:463` calls `Find.LetterStack.LetterStackTick()` — so it runs\n   inside `DoSingleTick`, which is exactly what `TimeDriver` drives.\n2. `LetterStack.LetterStackTick` (`Verse/LetterStack.cs:154`) calls\n   `OpenAutomaticLetters` every tick.\n3. `LetterWithTimeout.ShouldAutomaticallyOpenLetter =\u003e LastTickBeforeTimeout`\n   (`Verse/LetterWithTimeout.cs:35`). On its last tick, the letter opens ITSELF.\n4. `OpenLetter()` stacks a window; ~55 vanilla windows set `forcePause = true`.\n5. **`OpenAutomaticLetters` early-returns while any force-pausing window is up**\n   (`Verse/LetterStack.cs:139-142`, guarded on\n   `Find.WindowStack.WindowsForcePause`).\n\nVanilla survives this because a `forcePause` window actually pauses the game, so\nthe player deals with the modal and the queue drains. **We do not pause.**\n`TimeDriver` pins `CurTimeSpeed = Paused` and calls `DoSingleTick` itself, so\n`TickManager.Paused` is not what gates our loop, and\n`grep -n 'WindowStack' Source/AutoRimmer/*.cs` returns **nothing**. So the loop\nruns on with a modal stacked, and step 5 then swallows every subsequent\nauto-opening letter for the rest of the session.\n\n\nM1 is a ten-day unattended run. Timing-out letters are ordinary content — trade\ncaravan offers, quest offers, threats with a response window. The failure is\nsilent in the worst way: no exception, no red error, no halted advance. The run\nlooks healthy and the colony simply stops being told things.\n\nIt also means an `advance` can return with the game in a state no verb can\nclear, because nothing closes windows.\n\n\n- **An advance must not run under a force-pausing window.** Detect\n  `Find.WindowStack.WindowsForcePause` in the frame step and halt with a new\n  reason (`dialog`, alongside `ticks|letter|threat|alert|event|error|timeout`),\n  reporting which window(s) are up. Halting is the honest default: the game is\n  telling us a decision is owed.\n- **Standing invariant: `advance` returns with an empty force-pause stack**, or\n  it says so in its result. Same shape as the existing \"always returns paused\".\n- The window must be identifiable to the caller — type name at minimum — so 3.5\n  can route it and rwtest can assert on it.\n- Journal it. A modal opening mid-advance is a first-class event and currently\n  leaves no trace at all.\n- Do NOT close windows here. Deciding what a dialog means is 3.5's job; this\n  spec's job is to stop, report, and not corrupt the letter queue.\n\n\nWhether a non-force-pausing window should also halt (probably not);\nwhether to expose a `dismiss`/`close` escape hatch here or leave it wholly to\n3.5; whether the halt should be suppressible for a run that genuinely wants to\nplough on, and what that costs given step 5.\n\n\n- With a `LetterWithTimeout` driven to its last tick during an advance, the\n  advance halts with reason `dialog`, names the window, and the game is left\n  with the modal up rather than ticking beneath it.\n- After that halt, a second timing-out letter still opens when the stack is\n  clear — i.e. the queue was not poisoned. This is the regression the whole\n  issue exists for, so demonstrate it explicitly rather than by inference.\n- An advance over a window-free span is unchanged: same tick count, same\n  reason, no new overhead. Measure it, do not assert it.\n- Zero red errors.","files":null}]}